      "tool": {
        "driver": {
          "name": "Strix",
          "informationUri": "https://strix.ai",
          "rules": [
            {
              "id": "CWE-287",
              "name": "OAuth callback state is not bound to the initiating user in calendar integration",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "OAuth callback state is not bound to the initiating user in calendar integration"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The calendar OAuth callback accepts any request that presents a live `state` value and provider `code`, then binds the resulting calendar connection to the victim institute without verifying that the callback caller is the same authenticated administrator who initiated the flow. This allows cross-session callback completion and unauthorized provider-account binding if an attacker obtains a live `state` value before it is consumed."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The calendar OAuth callback accepts any request that presents a live `state` value and provider `code`, then binds the resulting calendar connection to the victim institute without verifying that the callback caller is the same authenticated administrator who initiated the flow. This allows cross-session callback completion and unauthorized provider-account binding if an attacker obtains a live `state` value before it is consumed.\n\nAn attacker who learns a valid in-flight calendar OAuth `state` value can complete the callback from a different browser session, including an unauthenticated one, and attach the attacker's Google or Microsoft calendar account to the victim institute's integration context. Because the callback marks `used_at` only after token exchange, connection update, and sync, there is also a replay/race window before the state is finalized.\n\n1. Require the callback to complete in the same authenticated application session that initiated the OAuth flow, rather than treating possession of the opaque `state` value as sufficient authorization.\n2. Compare the authenticated callback principal to `CalendarOAuthState.user` before exchanging the provider `code` or updating any institute connection.\n3. Consume the state atomically before token exchange and synchronization so concurrent callbacks cannot reuse the same live state.\n4. Fail closed when the callback is unauthenticated, when the authenticated user does not match the recorded initiator, or when the state has already been consumed.\n5. Consider binding the state to a signed nonce stored in the user's authenticated session in addition to the database row.",
                "markdown": "The calendar OAuth callback accepts any request that presents a live `state` value and provider `code`, then binds the resulting calendar connection to the victim institute without verifying that the callback caller is the same authenticated administrator who initiated the flow. This allows cross-session callback completion and unauthorized provider-account binding if an attacker obtains a live `state` value before it is consumed.\n\nAn attacker who learns a valid in-flight calendar OAuth `state` value can complete the callback from a different browser session, including an unauthenticated one, and attach the attacker's Google or Microsoft calendar account to the victim institute's integration context. Because the callback marks `used_at` only after token exchange, connection update, and sync, there is also a replay/race window before the state is finalized.\n\n1. Require the callback to complete in the same authenticated application session that initiated the OAuth flow, rather than treating possession of the opaque `state` value as sufficient authorization.\n2. Compare the authenticated callback principal to `CalendarOAuthState.user` before exchanging the provider `code` or updating any institute connection.\n3. Consume the state atomically before token exchange and synchronization so concurrent callbacks cannot reuse the same live state.\n4. Fail closed when the callback is unauthenticated, when the authenticated user does not match the recorded initiator, or when the state has already been consumed.\n5. Consider binding the state to a signed nonce stored in the user's authenticated session in addition to the database row."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "3.7",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-287",
                  "stride:S"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/287.html"
            }
          ],
          "version": "1.5.3"
        }
      },
